A||||6||Cliff Pennock||Tach filter
Z||000000||Cliff Pennock||04-01-99||10:06 AM||||Somebody I know just did an engine swap and replaced the stock V6 with a 3.4 V6. Now this engine gets much higher RPM than the stock engine and the problem is that the tachometer drops as soon as it hits between 6000-7000 rpm. I suspect he needs another tach filter (with a lower capacitor value). Can anyone confirm this? Or is there another reason why the tachometer may drop?
Z||000001||theogre||04-02-99||09:08 AM||||The tach filter, as you may know, is an RC (resistor capacitor) network. Depending on the values of the parts, it could be causing your problem. (I've yet to find a acurate schematic for it. Some "replacement" filters are a single resistor & cap. someone at GM once told me they are a couple cap's and 3 resistors, and this is how most of the manual's I've seen list them as well.)<p>The perpose of the filter, for readers who don't know, is to knock down stray noise on the tach feed, so that the tach only see's ignition pulses.<p>To test it, find an oscyloscope(sp) and watch the tach feed as the RPM's. if it's the tach filter, you'll see it on the screen. You'll then need someone to do some math, and recalc the RC values so that the new filter eliminates the drop out.<p>In it's simplest terms, the resistor controls how fast the cap charges, at high RPM the cap may not charge enough to pass the pulse to the tach. You could use a smaller cap, but you may increase the noise that gets thru too much. try reducing the resistance, which is more controlable, first. replace the resistor with a "trimmer" poptentiometer set to the same initial value. then tweek it down slightly, and see what you get.
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||04-02-99||09:31 AM||||I did connect an oscilloscope to my own Fiero's tach feed a few months ago (to test the digital dashboard), and I was suprised to see how "dirty" the signal was, because there was a lot of high frequency interference. I was suprised that the digital dash could get a reading <b>at all</b>...<p>I've been trying to find the capacitor and resistor values myself, so I can build a better one, but haven't been able to find it anywhere. Maybe I should give GM a call instead.
Z||000003||theogre||04-02-99||02:26 PM||||well this is one of the scematic I found on the web. For what it is.... I don't think the values are that critical, I think GM just needed to get it filtered enough....... seems like there are at least a dozen schematics people claim work. <p>As for the digital dash, I expect it's doing allot of it's own signal conditioning.<p>&gt;&gt;begin:<br>               ---------------------------------------------- &lt;----- tube (copper / steel ) <br>coil---------- resistor -------o----------------------------- tach <br>                                        | <br>                                      cap <br>                                       | <br>               -----------------o------------------------------ <p>The capacitor is soldered to the line from the resistor to the tach and to the tube (ground). <p>The parts you require are one .033 uf capacitor and one 1300 ohm resistor. <br>&gt;&gt;End:
Z||000004||theogre||04-02-99||02:27 PM||||move the cap to the 2 -o-'s. the BBS software didn't preserve the lead spaces.
Z||000005||GREENDANO||04-04-99||12:10 PM||||I have an exact wiring diagram with the correct values for the components.  If you want the picture E-mail me and I'll send it to you.<br> Dan-O
